virglrenderer: Venus image descriptions, binds and layout queries within the host's limits

What: vkr checks what a guest's image calls would hand to the Mac's Vulkan
driver before it does (src/venus/vkr_image.c):
- vkCreateImage, vkGetDeviceImageMemoryRequirements,
  vkGetDeviceImageSparseMemoryRequirements and vkGetDeviceImageSubresourceLayout
  take the create info the way vkCreateImage changes it (dma-buf handles, then the
  emulated LINEAR modifier, then the check; the three queries never had the
  modifier step and gave the host DRM tiling). The check: image type, tiling, one
  sample count, no zero sizes, a 1D or 2D shape, cube compatible = 2D square with 6+
  layers, only flag and usage bits vulkan_core.h names; then the driver's own
  vkGetPhysicalDeviceImageFormatProperties2 for exactly that format, type, tiling,
  usage and flags (a new question: external memory and format list stay out,
  KosmicKrisp refuses MTLTEXTURE handles there; LINEAR asks what
  virgl-venus-linear-modifier-usage.patch asks) must succeed, and extent, levels,
  layers and samples must be in its answer, levels also <= log2(largest side) + 1.
- The guest-facing format queries (both versions, and the sparse ones) refuse
  an image type or tiling outside the spec; KosmicKrisp's query ends in
  UNREACHABLE for them (undefined in its release build).
- vkBindImageMemory(2): the memory's type is one of the image's, the offset is
  aligned, the image (per plane for DISJOINT images) ends inside the memory. vkr
  keeps each image's type, format, levels, layers and the host's memory
  requirements (one query at create).
- vkGetImageSubresourceLayout(2) and vkGetImageMemoryRequirements2: an aspect,
  level, layer and plane of the image.
- vkGetImageSubresourceLayout2 and the three vkGetDeviceImage* queries only on a
  device that has them: a guest can send any command, and for an API version or
  extension the device was not made with vkr called a NULL entry point.
A refused create returns VK_ERROR_FORMAT_NOT_SUPPORTED (what the format query
gives for the same values; not in vkCreateImage's list in the spec, but vkr
already returns it for a DRM modifier it cannot make, and guest Mesa passes it to
the app). A refused query zeroes only its main output (VkMemoryRequirements,
VkSubresourceLayout, VkImageFormatProperties; a sparse count of 0), and the format
queries also return VK_ERROR_FORMAT_NOT_SUPPORTED. Output structs chained to it
(VkMemoryDedicatedRequirements, VkExternalImageFormatProperties,
VkSubresourceHostMemcpySize) are not written and keep what the decoder's temp
pool held (follow-up #624). Binds are made without waiting for an
answer in the guest, so a refused bind ends the app's Venus context. The verdict
depends only on the device and the description. The guest sends a create without
waiting only when the same description is in its requirements cache (a
successful create or a requirements query before); then the app's first use of
the image ends its context. QEMU's log names the app, the call, the values and the
limit at the 1st, 2nd, 4th ... refusal (vkr_context_refuse, one atomic count).
src/venus/vkr_format_table.h (texel block size, planes, aspects) is generated
by Tests/virgl/venus-format-table.py from the Vulkan registry. Uses
vkr_physical_device_linear_usage from virgl-venus-linear-modifier-usage.patch.
Scope: what the Mac drivers (Metal under them) end the process on, not every
valid-usage rule.
Why: Metal checks texture descriptors in release builds too and ends the
process (MTLReportFailure, abort) on what it cannot make; KosmicKrisp and
MoltenVK pass guest values on to it. Venus runs in QEMU's process on macOS
(virgl-darwin-venus-in-process.patch), so any program in the VM could stop the
VM: too many levels or layers, a texture past 16384 texels, a sample count the
format has not, a zero size, a linear depth or compressed image (KosmicKrisp),
a 2D image with depth 4 (MoltenVK), PLANE_1 of a one-plane image in
vkGetImageSubresourceLayout (MoltenVK crashes), a format a driver has no entry
for. A bind past the memory's end leaves KosmicKrisp a linear image without a
texture for the GPU to use later.
Upstream: OmacVM policy. Upstream virglrenderer runs the render server as its own
process, where a driver abort ends that process only (ADR 0065).
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-venus-image-limits.c (build time): every probe case
through vkr's own dispatch with the real drivers, one child process per case;
before the patch the cases end the child, after it they get the error or a
context end, and the must-keep cases (window buffers, optimal 2D, array, cube, 3D,
multisampled, mutable sRGB storage images) still create and bind. A refused
description twice, then a use of its handle: the context ends, the driver is never
called.

diff --git a/src/venus/vkr_context.c b/src/venus/vkr_context.c
index c4e9cea..c3f51de 100644
--- a/src/venus/vkr_context.c
+++ b/src/venus/vkr_context.c
@@ -5,6 +5,8 @@
 
 #include "vkr_context.h"
 
+#include <stdarg.h>
+#include <stdio.h>
 #include <sys/mman.h>
 #include <sys/types.h>
 #include <unistd.h>
@@ -508,6 +510,28 @@ vkr_context_get_wait_ring_seqno(struct vkr_context *ctx,
    return wait_ring;
 }
 
+void
+vkr_context_refuse(struct vkr_context *ctx, bool fatal, const char *call, const char *fmt, ...)
+{
+   /* one count for the process: ring threads of several apps refuse at the same time */
+   static atomic_uint_fast64_t refusals;
+   const uint64_t n = atomic_fetch_add(&refusals, 1) + 1;
+
+   /* the 1st, 2nd, 4th, 8th ... refusal: a guest in a loop cannot flood the log */
+   if (!(n & (n - 1))) {
+      char why[256];
+      va_list va;
+      va_start(va, fmt);
+      vsnprintf(why, sizeof(why), fmt, va);
+      va_end(va);
+      vkr_log("%s: %s refused: %s%s (%" PRIu64 " refused so far)",
+              ctx->debug_name ? ctx->debug_name : "?", call, why,
+              fatal ? "; the app's Vulkan context ends" : "", n);
+   }
+   if (fatal)
+      vkr_context_set_fatal(ctx);
+}
+
 void
 vkr_context_on_ring_fatal(struct vkr_context *ctx)
 {
